"""Binary/backend/toolchain selector bypass for the light-daemon lane.

Importable on purpose, for the same reason `daemon_backlog.spl` and
`no_examples_gate.spl` are: `test_runner_client.spl` is a standalone script
with a bare `fn main` and cannot be imported by a spec (documented fn-main
collision), so the DECISION lives here where a unit spec can pin it and the
client keeps only the I/O.

WHY THIS BYPASS EXISTS

Bug: light_daemon_cannot_serve_pinned_native_exec_2026-11-03. The light
daemon's v2 request (light_protocol.spl) carries a path, expiry, an
environment digest and a generation, but NO binary and no execution mode,
and the daemon is pinned for life to the binary that spawned it with its
environment frozen at spawn time. A run that pins WHICH binary, backend or
toolchain the spec exercises (the selector family below) therefore can never
be served by the light daemon. Recorded decision: KEEP the documented bypass
(pin: test/01_unit/app/test_daemon/light_daemon_pinned_bypass_spec.spl) —
the client diverts every pinned run to the direct lane, where the caller's
own environment reaches the spec. The sibling session daemon solves the same
problem with an explicit native profile (src/app/test_daemon/
native_profile.spl, PR #5407); the light lane deliberately does not.

# Environment variables by which a caller selects WHICH binary, backend or
# toolchain a spec exercises. These are the names specs resolve in a
# `contract_binary()` / `simple_binary()` helper; see
# test/01_unit/lib/mem_infra/guard_backend_parity_spec.spl and its siblings.
# None of them is set in a normal run, so this list is inert on the hot path and
# costs the daemon lane nothing — it only diverts the runs that actually asked
# for an override.
#
# The list must stay CLOSED over the family. The daemon freezes its environment
# at whichever invocation started it, so any selector NOT named here is silently
# replayed from a previous run and the spec exercises the default target while
# reporting the selected one's name — a sabotage check then passes for the wrong
# reason. Measured 2026-08-04 on the Rust seed (`bin/simple`; bootstrap-identity
# probe = 0), with one daemon alive across all three runs and
# test/fixtures/mem_infra/env_selector_probe_spec.spl as the target:
#
#   run 1: <six sibling selectors>=BRAVO    body observed BRAVO   (this run
#                                           STARTED the daemon — the trap: a
#                                           single run always looks correct)
#   run 2: same six =CHARLIE                body observed BRAVO   <- DEAD
#   run 3: same six =DELTA + SIMPLE_TEST_BINARY=DELTA
#                                           body observed DELTA   <- bypass works
#
# Run 3 differs from run 2 only in naming an allowlisted variable, so membership
# in this list is exactly what makes a selector live.
"""

use std.nogc_sync_mut.io.env_ops.{env_get}

fn binary_override_selector_vars() -> [text]:
    ["SIMPLE_TEST_BINARY", "SIMPLE_BINARY", "SIMPLE_BIN",
     "SIMPLE_SEED_BINARY", "SIMPLE_SPEC_COMPILER",
     # sibling selectors, added 2026-08-04 after the probe above
     "CPU_SIMD_RENDER_SCALE_TEST_SIMPLE_BIN",
     "SIMPLEOS_QEMU_SIMPLE_BIN", "SIMPLEOS_DBFS_BOOT_QEMU_EXEC",
     "SIMPLEOS_QEMU_SSH_TOOLCHAIN_LIVE",
     "SIMPLE_HOSTED_BROWSER_EXECUTABLE", "DEVHUB_MAIL_BIN",
     "T32_PYTHON_BINARY", "T32_BACKEND_PREFERENCE", "LLVM_BUILD",
     "SIMPLE_MMU_DIRECT_BACKEND", "SIMPLE_MMU_DEVICE_INITIATED_BACKEND",
     "SIMPLE_WEB_GPU_PAINT_MEASURE_BACKEND", "SIMPLE_NATIVE_BUILD_TARGET",
     "SIMPLE_ENGINE2D_RUNNER_MODE", "SIMPLE_ENGINE2D_FULL_MODE",
     # the GPU-compiler provenance family travels together: the *_PATH/PRODUCER
     # names select the artifact, the *_SHA256 names are the expected values
     # checked against it, and a half-frozen pair is worse than a frozen one.
     "SIMPLE_GPU_COMPILER_PRODUCER", "SIMPLE_GPU_COMPILER_PRODUCER_SHA256",
     "SIMPLE_GPU_COMPILER_RUNTIME_PATH", "SIMPLE_GPU_COMPILER_RUNTIME_SHA256",
     "SIMPLE_GPU_COMPILER_BACKFILL_SHA256",
     # PERF-7, 2026-09-13: a perf spec resolves the interpreter it measures
     # from SIMPLE_PERF_SELF_BIN. It is a binary selector exactly like the
     # names above, and it was missing here, so a daemon-served run answered
     # from the daemon's FROZEN environment: a lane asked for the BASE seed,
     # got the CANDIDATE, and reported 10/10 green for a spec that is RED on
     # base. Caught only because the spec prints the binary it resolved.
     "SIMPLE_PERF_SELF_BIN"]

# The decision, pure: which selectors are pinned, given (name, value) pairs.
# Order follows the pair input; the env wrapper below supplies family order
# so the bypass message is deterministic.
# A selector is "set" when its value is non-empty — "" is the only spelling
# of "not set" across every consumer of these names.
fn binary_override_active_selectors(pairs: [(text, text)]) -> [text]:
    var active: [text] = []
    for pair in pairs:
        val (name, value) = pair
        if value != "":
            active.push(name)
    active

fn binary_override_env_selectors() -> [text]:
    var pairs: [(text, text)] = []
    for name in binary_override_selector_vars():
        pairs.push((name, env_get(name) ?? ""))
    binary_override_active_selectors(pairs)

fn has_binary_override() -> bool:
    binary_override_env_selectors().len() > 0

fn binary_override_names() -> text:
    binary_override_env_selectors().join(", ")

export binary_override_selector_vars
export binary_override_active_selectors
export binary_override_env_selectors
export has_binary_override
export binary_override_names
